{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Calculate ac (multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant)",
   "Find two numbers that multiply to ac and add to b",
   "Split the middle term bx into two terms using these numbers",
   "Group into pairs, factorise each, then take out the common bracket"
  ],
  "title": "How to Factorise Quadratics Where a ≠ 1",
  "content": "<p>For quadratics of the form \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\) where \\(a \\neq 1\\), you cannot simply find two numbers that add to \\(b\\) and multiply to \\(c\\). Instead, find two numbers that multiply to \\(ac\\) and add to \\(b\\).</p><p><strong>Method (splitting the middle term):</strong></p><p>1. Multiply \\(a \\times c\\)</p><p>2. Find two numbers that multiply to \\(ac\\) and add to \\(b\\)</p><p>3. Rewrite the middle term as two separate terms using these numbers</p><p>4. Group into pairs and factorise each pair</p><p>5. Take out the common bracket</p><p><strong>Alternative:</strong> Use the grid/box method or trial and improvement with double brackets.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Factorise</strong> \\(6x^2 + 11x + 3\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> \\(ac = 6 \\times 3 = 18\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Two numbers: \\(9\\) and \\(2\\) (multiply to 18, add to 11)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> \\(6x^2 + 9x + 2x + 3\\)</p><p><strong>Step 4:</strong> \\(3x(2x + 3) + 1(2x + 3) = (3x + 1)(2x + 3)\\)</p>"
 },
